I'm back.

It's been more than three months since I last continued learning Swift, and honestly, I didn't look back at it during that time.

I had lost motivation. A big reason was that I wasn't sure if I was going to get a Mac of my own in the future. Without knowing that, it was difficult to convince myself to keep going with something I wasn't even sure I'd be able to continue properly.

Eventually, I just stopped.

But now, things are different. I finally got myself a MacBook Air M5, and somehow, that lost spirit is back.

Before jumping straight into the next lesson, I went back and read my own previous writings. It had been so long that I needed to refresh my memory, and honestly, going through those posts helped more than I expected. Things I'd learned earlier started coming back to me, and concepts that felt a little distant became easier to understand again.

It felt good to revisit something I'd written myself and realize that it was actually helping me learn all over again.

Yesterday, I finally completed Day 7 of 100 Days of SwiftUI.

Today's lesson was about functions, parameters, return values, tuples, and parameter labels. It was a little different from the previous lessons because instead of focusing on repeating work or making decisions, I was learning how to organize code into reusable pieces.

## Teaching Swift to Reuse Code

One thing I've started noticing while learning programming is how quickly code can become repetitive.

Imagine writing the same ten lines of code in several different places.

It might work perfectly fine.

But what happens when I need to change something?

I'd have to find every place where I wrote that code and update it individually. And if I missed one, things could start behaving differently.

That's where functions come in.

Instead of repeating the same instructions, I can define them once and use them whenever I need them.

```swift
func showWelcome() {
    print("Welcome to my app!")
    print("Thanks for being here.")
}

showWelcome()
```

The `func` keyword tells Swift that I'm creating a function.

The name describes what it does, and the braces contain the instructions that should run when I call it.

One thing worth understanding is that defining a function doesn't automatically execute its code.

I have to call it using its name followed by parentheses.

```swift
showWelcome()
```

That distinction is surprisingly important.

I can define what needs to happen first and decide when it should happen later.

It makes the code easier to organize, and I don't have to keep writing the same thing over and over again.

## Giving Functions Some Flexibility

A function that always does exactly the same thing is useful, but what if I want to change its behavior without rewriting it?

That's where parameters become important.

Instead of creating a separate function for every times table, I can let the function accept a number.

```swift
func printTimesTables(number: Int) {
    for i in 1...12 {
        print("\(i) x \(number) is \(i * number)")
    }
}

printTimesTables(number: 5)
printTimesTables(number: 8)
```

Now the same function works for different numbers.

The `number: Int` part defines a parameter. It tells Swift that the function expects an integer, and I can use the value inside the function just like any other constant.

When I call the function, I provide an actual value, known as an argument.

For example, in `printTimesTables(number: 5)`, the argument is `5`.

The distinction is simple: a parameter is the placeholder, while an argument is the actual value being passed in.

I can also add more than one parameter.

```swift
func printTimesTables(number: Int, end: Int) {
    for i in 1...end {
        print("\(i) x \(number) is \(i * number)")
    }
}

printTimesTables(number: 5, end: 20)
```

This time, I can control both the number whose times table I want and how far it should go.

Something else I learned is that Swift expects arguments to be provided in the same order as the parameters are defined.

Since `number` comes before `end`, I can't simply switch their positions when calling the function.

The labels make it easier to understand what each value represents, even when a function accepts multiple parameters.

## Getting Values Back from Functions

Until now, I'd mostly thought about functions as something that performs an action.

Print something.

Calculate something.

Repeat something.

But functions can also return values that I can use elsewhere in my code.

```swift
func rollDice() -> Int {
    return Int.random(in: 1...6)
}

let result = rollDice()
print(result)
```

The output will be a random integer between 1 and 6.

The `-> Int` tells Swift that the function returns an integer.

The `return` keyword sends the calculated value back to the place where the function was called.

Instead of printing the result from inside the function, I can store it in a constant and use it later.

That feels like a much more flexible way of working.

One detail I found interesting is that Swift can sometimes figure out when a value should be returned without explicitly writing `return`.

For example:

```swift
func square(number: Int) -> Int {
    number * number
}

let result = square(number: 5)
print(result)
```

The output is:

```text
25
```

Because the function contains a single expression, Swift can infer that its result should be returned.

This makes simple functions much cleaner to write.

I also learned that functions don't always need to return a value. In some situations, using `return` by itself lets a function exit early when there's no reason to continue executing its remaining instructions.

The important thing is understanding what a function promises to return and making sure the code fulfils that promise.

## Putting Functions to Work

One example from the lesson that helped me connect everything was checking whether two strings contain the same letters, regardless of their order.

For example, `"abc"` and `"cab"` contain the same letters, even though they are arranged differently.

I can use `sorted()` to arrange the letters alphabetically and then compare the results.

```swift
func areLettersIdentical(string1: String, string2: String) -> Bool {
    string1.sorted() == string2.sorted()
}

print(areLettersIdentical(string1: "abc", string2: "cab"))
print(areLettersIdentical(string1: "hello", string2: "world"))
```

The output is:

```text
true
false
```

The function accepts two strings and returns a Boolean value.

It doesn't need to print the answer itself. It simply calculates the result and returns either `true` or `false`.

This is where the earlier concepts started coming together for me.

I'm passing information into a function, using that information to perform an operation, and getting a result back.

It's a simple example, but it shows how much can be done with just a few lines of code.

## Returning More Than One Value

This was probably one of the more interesting concepts from today's lesson.

What happens when a function needs to return multiple related values?

I could use an array.

I could use a dictionary.

But both approaches have limitations depending on what I'm trying to do.

With an array, I'd need to remember which index represents which value. With a dictionary, I'd have to access values using keys and account for the possibility that a key might not exist.

Swift offers another option: tuples.

```swift
func getUser() -> (firstName: String, lastName: String) {
    ("Taylor", "Swift")
}

let user = getUser()

print("Name: \(user.firstName) \(user.lastName)")
```

Instead of returning a single string, the function returns two strings together as a tuple.

The interesting part is that I can give each value its own name.

That means I can access `user.firstName` and `user.lastName` instead of remembering which value belongs at which index.

Swift already knows the names from the return type, so I don't even need to repeat them inside the tuple returned by the function.

I can also use a tuple without naming its elements.

```swift
func getUser() -> (String, String) {
    ("Taylor", "Swift")
}

let user = getUser()

print(user.0)
print(user.1)
```

Here, `.0` accesses the first value and `.1` accesses the second.

It works, but I find the named version much easier to read.

Another useful feature is that I can extract the values directly into separate constants.

```swift
let (firstName, lastName) = getUser()
```

And if I only need one of them, I can ignore the other using `_`.

```swift
let (firstName, _) = getUser()
```

This is one of those features that makes more sense when I see it in actual code.

I don't need to create a complicated structure just to return two related values.

Sometimes, a tuple is all I need.

## Making Function Calls Read Naturally

The final part of today's lesson was about parameter labels.

I'd already learned how to pass information into functions. Now I was learning that the way I call a function doesn't always have to match the name I use for its parameter inside the function.

Consider this:

```swift
func printTimesTables(number: Int) {
    for i in 1...12 {
        print("\(i) x \(number) is \(i * number)")
    }
}

printTimesTables(number: 5)
```

There's nothing wrong with that code.

But Swift allows me to make the function call read more naturally.

```swift
func printTimesTables(for number: Int) {
    for i in 1...12 {
        print("\(i) x \(number) is \(i * number)")
    }
}

printTimesTables(for: 5)
```

Now the call reads almost like plain English.

Print times tables for five.

The interesting part is that `for` is the external parameter label, while `number` is the name I use inside the function.

I can use one name when calling the function and another when working with the value itself.

Swift also lets me remove the external label entirely using an underscore.

```swift
func isUppercase(_ string: String) -> Bool {
    string == string.uppercased()
}

let result = isUppercase("HELLO")
print(result)
```

Without the underscore, I'd need to write `isUppercase(string: "HELLO")`.

With it, the call becomes simpler.

This is used in many places throughout Swift. For example, when adding an item to an array, I can write `append("Swift")` rather than having to provide an extra parameter label.

I like how these small choices can make code easier to read without changing what it actually does.

## Debugging My Own Thinking

One thing I noticed while going through today's checkpoints was that understanding the syntax wasn't always enough.

I could recognize what a function did, but I still needed to understand how its inputs and outputs worked together.

What value is being passed in?

What does the function do with it?

What value comes back?

Which name should I use when calling it?

Those questions helped me approach the code more carefully.

Instead of looking at a function as one big block of instructions, I started breaking it down into smaller parts.

The parameters provide the input.

The function performs its work.

The return value provides the output, when one is needed.

Once I started thinking about functions that way, the different concepts became easier to connect.

It's a similar feeling to what I experienced while learning loops.

Sometimes, the difficult part isn't the syntax.

It's understanding exactly what the code is doing.

## Patterns Are Starting to Appear

Looking back at the previous lessons, I'm beginning to see how the concepts fit together.

Variables store information.

Collections organize related values.

Conditions decide what should happen.

Loops repeat work.

Functions package that work into reusable pieces.

Parameters let functions accept information, and return values let them give information back.

Tuples allow multiple related values to be returned together, while parameter labels make function calls easier to understand.

Every new lesson seems to build on something I learned earlier.

Instead of learning completely separate features, I'm slowly learning how to combine them.

And I think that's when programming starts becoming more interesting.

## Finding My Motivation Again

Coming back to Swift after more than three months felt a little strange.

I hadn't been practising in the background or occasionally checking what I'd missed. I had genuinely stopped learning and hadn't looked back at Swift during that time.

That's why going through my old blog posts helped.

I'd written those posts while learning the concepts for the first time, so revisiting them felt different from simply reading a tutorial again. I could see how I'd understood things back then, and it helped me get back into the same mindset.

It also reminded me why I'd started this journey in the first place.

For a while, I genuinely wasn't sure whether I'd get a Mac of my own. That uncertainty slowly took away my motivation, and eventually, I stopped learning Swift altogether.

Now that I finally have my MacBook Air M5, I feel like I have something to look forward to again.

I'm not suddenly an expert, and there's still a lot I need to learn. But I'm enjoying the process again, and I think that's what matters right now.

Completing Day 7 might not sound like a huge achievement, but after leaving Swift untouched for so long, it feels good to finally be moving forward again.

## Looking Ahead

Today's lesson wasn't just about functions.

It was about learning how to structure code, reuse logic, and make different parts of a program work together.

I'm still early in this journey, and I know there are many concepts I haven't explored yet.

But I'm beginning to understand that writing good code isn't only about making something work.

It's also about making it reusable, understandable, and easy to work with later.

I'm glad I went back and read my old writings instead of simply trying to pick up where I left off. Sometimes, revisiting what I've already learned is exactly what I need to move forward.

After a break of more than three months, I'm finally back.

Seven days completed, a MacBook Air M5 on my desk, and the motivation to keep learning again.

Let's see how far I can go this time.

